# CHECKPOINT 01

## From Cognition to the Formation of a Point of View

### Status

This checkpoint records a genuine advance in the enquiry.

We began by asking whether cognition is intrinsically self-validating. That question depended on an overly simple ontology:

[
\text{subject}
\rightarrow
\text{cognition}
\rightarrow
\text{object}.
]

The investigation has dissolved that model.

What we ordinarily call a cognition—“I see a bottle”—is not plausibly a single, sharply bounded event in which a finished subject receives a finished object. It is a stable higher-level pattern generated within temporally extended, nested organism–environment dynamics.

This does not defeat Abhinavagupta. It forces his argument deeper.

The live contention is no longer:

> Does cognition possess reflexive awareness?

It is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{What makes a nested process one point of view from which a world manifests?}
}
]

---

# I. The original model has been rejected

The naive model was:

[
S\overset{C}{\longrightarrow}O
]

where:

* (S) is an already constituted subject;
* (C) is one completed cognition;
* (O) is an already constituted object.

Applied to perception:

[
\text{subject sees bottle}.
]

This model assumes what must be explained:

1. what individuates the subject;
2. what individuates the cognition;
3. what makes the bottle one object;
4. how these three become related;
5. why the relation is manifest.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{subject, cognition and object cannot initially be treated as primitives.}
}
]

---

# II. Perception is active stabilization

Predictive-processing models treat perception as an active generative process. Hierarchical predictions are constrained by sensory prediction errors, while action changes the sensory information subsequently received.

The experienced bottle is therefore not a passive copy of an external object:

[
\text{bottle experience}
\neq
\text{sensory imprint}.
]

Nor is it an unconstrained fabrication:

[
\text{bottle experience}
\neq
\text{free hallucination}.
]

It is a dynamically stabilized hypothesis:

[
B_t
===

f(M_t,S_t,A_t,H),
]

where:

* (M_t) is the current generative model;
* (S_t) is sensory constraint;
* (A_t) is active sampling;
* (H) is the organism’s developmental and learning history.

Seth’s work on perceptual presence proposes that an object feels present partly because the perceptual model contains counterfactual expectations about how sensory input would change under possible movements and interactions. The bottle is experienced as objectively there because the system implicitly models a structured range of possible encounters with it. ([PubMed][1])

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{perceptual presence is enacted across possible interactions, not delivered in one sensory instant.}
}
]

---

# III. There may be no fundamental moment of cognition

A cognition such as “seeing the bottle” can be represented as a trajectory:

[
\Gamma_{B}
==========

{
M_t,S_t,A_t,E_t
}_{t_0}^{t_n},
]

where (E_t) represents prediction-error and updating dynamics.

No unique instant need satisfy:

[
t^\ast
======

\text{the exact moment the bottle cognition exists}.
]

The boundaries of the cognition may be scale-relative:

* retinal processing;
* sensorimotor coordination;
* object stabilization;
* conceptual identification;
* memory encoding;
* verbal report.

Consequently:

[
\boxed{
\text{a cognition can be real without being fundamentally discrete.}
}
]

This undermines any argument that begins by treating “a cognition” as an ontologically atomic item.

---

# IV. Wallace supplies the ontology of real patterns

David Wallace’s Everettian structural ontology rejects the choice between:

[
\text{fundamental object}
]

and:

[
\text{mere illusion}.
]

Macroscopic objects and quantum branches can be objectively real as dynamically robust patterns emergent through decoherence, even when they are not basic objects in the microscopic formalism. ([OUP Academic][2])

His later work generalizes the point: ontology at different scientific scales can consist of real patterns grounded in underlying mathematical structure. ([PhilSci Archive][3])

Applied to cognition:

[
\text{seeing the bottle}
]

need not be a primitive neural event.

It can be a real, explanatory pattern in a larger process.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
\text{lack of an exact boundary does not imply unreality.}
}
]

A bottle, organism, self or cognition may be real when the pattern:

* remains sufficiently stable;
* supports successful prediction;
* participates in causal explanations;
* exhibits partial dynamical autonomy;
* compresses the underlying complexity.

This resolves the false dilemma:

[
\text{atomic cognition}
\quad\text{or}\quad
\text{no cognition}.
]

The cognition is real at its appropriate scale.

---

# V. Levin explains how cognitive scales form

Michael Levin’s work provides a model for how smaller competent agents combine into agents with larger goals.

Cells possess capacities for:

* sensing;
* memory;
* error correction;
* preference;
* homeostatic regulation;
* action in physiological and morphological problem spaces.

Multicellular intelligence emerges when communication mechanisms—especially bioelectric networks—allow cellular competencies to coordinate around large-scale goals that belong to the collective and not to any isolated cell. ([Springer][4])

The relevant transition is:

[
{g_1,g_2,\ldots,g_n}
\longrightarrow
G,
]

where (g_i) are local cellular goals and (G) is a larger anatomical or behavioural goal.

The larger organism is therefore not merely a pile of cells. It is a new control scale.

Levin’s “cognitive light cone” tracks how far through space, time and possible states a system can sense, remember and work toward goals. Recent work continues to treat cognition as graded and substrate-independent rather than as something that suddenly appears with brains. ([arXiv][5])

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{selves can be produced by the scaling and coordination of smaller competencies.}
}
]

But Levin does not yet establish:

[
\boxed{
\text{why the resulting collective is phenomenally manifest.}
}
]

He advances the problem of agency and individuation, not the final problem of manifestation.

---

# VI. The bottle is the terminus of many nested histories

The perceived bottle depends simultaneously on:

[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{physical interaction},\
&\text{cellular regulation},\
&\text{embodied sensorimotor capacities},\
&\text{neural generative modelling},\
&\text{developmental learning},\
&\text{social and linguistic categorization},\
&\text{present goals and affect}.
\end{aligned}
]

Therefore the perceived bottle cannot be cleanly detached from the processes producing it.

But neither is it reducible to any single one of them.

[
\boxed{
B_{\text{experienced}}
======================

\text{a stable actionable pattern across nested processes}.
}
]

“Bottle” is the scale at which an enormous causal history becomes available for coordinated action.

It is not the final static image generated after processing.

It is an active achievement continuously maintained by the system.

---

# VII. Ñāṇananda independently reaches a closely related structure

Bhikkhu Ñāṇananda’s analysis is strongly relevant, though it should not be equated with predictive processing.

His account emphasizes the reciprocal dependence of consciousness and name-and-form, and the way conceptual proliferation constructs an apparently stable, appropriable world. The Nibbāna sermons interpret consciousness not as an independent witness standing outside experience, but as caught within a mutually sustaining process with *nāma-rūpa*. ([The Open Buddhist University][6])

The especially relevant sequence is:

[
\text{contact}
\rightarrow
\text{feeling}
\rightarrow
\text{perception}
\rightarrow
\text{thinking}
\rightarrow
\textit{papañca}.
]

Ñāṇananda interprets *papañca* dynamically: experience becomes elaborated, spread out and appropriated through craving, conceit and views. His analysis stresses that ordinary concepts are not neutral descriptions; their apparent independent reality becomes “tinctured” by these affective and self-referential tendencies. ([Seeing Through The Net][7])

The notion of *nimitta*, or sign, is also relevant. A sign is the selected general appearance through which something becomes recognizable and significant. Attention to signs and secondary characteristics can lead into attraction, rejection, conceptualization and proliferation. ([Encyclopedia of Buddhism][8])

Thus Ñāṇananda’s model can be reconstructed as:

[
\text{sensory process}
\rightarrow
\text{selected sign}
\rightarrow
\text{affective significance}
\rightarrow
\text{appropriation}
\rightarrow
\text{apparently stable object-world}.
]

This resembles an affectively weighted attractor:

[
\boxed{
\text{a sign stabilizes because the organism’s history, craving, aversion and identity repeatedly privilege it.}
}
]

The correspondence is not identity:

[
\textit{nimitta}
\neq
\text{predictive representation}
]

and:

[
\textit{papañca}
\neq
\text{prediction error minimization}.
]

The shared structure is narrower:

[
\boxed{
\text{the determinate object is actively selected, stabilized and affectively charged rather than passively received.}
}
]

Ñāṇananda goes further than standard predictive processing in identifying how craving, conceit and views turn useful perceptual distinctions into an appropriated world:

[
\text{“this is significant”}
\rightarrow
\text{“this concerns me”}
\rightarrow
\text{“this is mine or threatens me”}.
]

He is therefore highly relevant to the transition from:

[
\text{stable perceptual pattern}
]

to:

[
\text{personally charged world}.
]

---

# VIII. What has been established about cognition

The earlier cognition debate can now be closed provisionally.

## Result 1

Cognition is not adequately modelled as passive representation.

[
\boxed{
C\neq\text{copying}.
}
]

## Result 2

Cognition is not necessarily a fundamental discrete event.

[
\boxed{
C\neq\text{atomic moment}.
}
]

## Result 3

Cognition is a temporally extended, active organism–environment pattern.

[
\boxed{
C\approx\text{active stabilization across nested processes}.
}
]

## Result 4

Objects of cognition are real at an emergent scale without being fundamental objects.

[
\boxed{
O_{\text{experienced}}
======================

\text{real actionable pattern}.
}
]

## Result 5

Individual cognition depends on collective biological intelligence.

[
\boxed{
\text{organismic cognition}
\leftarrow
\text{coordinated subagent competencies}.
}
]

## Result 6

Perceptual determination is affected by the organism’s goals, history and affective valuation.

[
\boxed{
\text{world-as-experienced}
===========================

\text{world constrained by situated significance}.
}
]

These results eliminate a large class of theories that presuppose:

* ready-made objects entering the mind;
* a detached inner spectator;
* a single point where perception becomes cognition;
* a fixed subject existing independently of its constituting processes;
* concepts as neutral copies of external reality.

---

# IX. What this does to Dharmakīrti

Dharmakīrti already rejects the ordinary bottle as an ultimately perceived entity.

The conceptual bottle is constructed over causally effective particulars through memory, exclusion and conceptual determination.

This gives him an important advantage over naive realism:

[
\text{perception}
\neq
\text{perception of the universal “bottle.”}
]

However, Dharmakīrti retains momentary cognitions as fundamental units.

The process account now pressures that commitment:

[
\boxed{
\text{Why posit atomic cognitive moments rather than temporally extended cognitive patterns?}
}
]

A causal stream of moments may itself be a theoretical coarse-graining of continuous dynamics.

Dharmakīrti remains powerful concerning:

* construction of object identity;
* nonconceptual versus conceptual cognition;
* causal continuity without enduring substance;
* the critique of a permanent knower.

He is now pressured at:

* the individuation of cognitive moments;
* the temporal scale of reflexivity;
* whether momentariness is discovered or imposed;
* how local cognitive moments form one operative point of view.

---

# X. What this does to Abhinavagupta

The weaker Pratyabhijñā formulation was:

[
C=P+V,
]

where cognition consists of manifestation plus reflexive apprehension.

This formulation is too static if it suggests a completed cognitive item possessing *vimarśa* as a property.

The process-compatible reconstruction is stronger:

[
\boxed{
\textit{vimarśa}
================

\text{the active differentiation, stabilization, integration and self-articulation of manifestation}.
}
]

On this reading, predictive processing, enaction, collective intelligence and emergent real-pattern ontology do not refute *vimarśa*.

They begin describing what *vimarśa* would have to look like when expressed through nested finite systems:

* differences become stabilized;
* boundaries become functional;
* local competencies become collective agency;
* objects become actionable;
* self-world models persist through change;
* a field becomes organized relative to a point of view.

This is a legitimate structural correspondence, not yet a proof of Śaiva metaphysics.

It permits the following advance:

[
\boxed{
\text{Science increasingly explains the organization of determinate cognition.}
}
]

In Śaiva terms:

[
\boxed{
\text{science is beginning to describe finite structures of }vimarśa.
}
]

But this intensifies rather than resolves Abhinavagupta’s deepest question.

If all objects, selves and cognitions are emergent nested patterns, then:

> Why is this the form manifestation takes?

And more fundamentally:

> Why is any such process manifest?

---

# XI. Manifestation has not been reduced to cognition

Three relations remain possible.

## Identity

[
M=C.
]

Manifestation simply is active cognition viewed first-personally.

Problem:

[
\text{What makes some active processes first-personal?}
]

## Emergence

[
R\rightarrow C\rightarrow M.
]

Non-conscious reality forms cognitive processes, which eventually generate manifestation.

Problem:

[
\text{What explains the transition from non-manifest organization to manifest organization?}
]

## Priority

[
M\rightarrow C.
]

Manifestation is prior, while cognition is one form of its active differentiation.

Problem:

[
\text{Why must manifestation possess this differentiating, perspectival and agent-forming structure?}
]

None has yet been proven.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{cognition has been structurally reconstructed, but manifestation has not been explained.}
}
]

---

# XII. Observer language cannot solve manifestation

The claim:

> Manifestation is cognition from the observer’s perspective

merely relocates the problem.

If the observer is another process:

[
C_1\text{ appears to }C_2,
]

then what makes (C_2) manifest?

If the observer is the complete system, we need a criterion for why this system forms one observer.

If “observer” means only the first-person description of the process, we still need to explain why a first-person description exists.

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{observerhood cannot explain manifestation until observerhood is itself explained.}
}
]

---

# XIII. Checkpoint theorem

The combined result is:

[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Cognitions, objects and selves are real, scale-relative patterns}\
&\text{formed through continuous nested organism–world processes.}
\end{aligned}
}
]

Seth explains how an actionable perceptual world is predictively stabilized.

Levin explains how smaller competencies combine into larger agents and goal spaces.

Wallace explains how higher-level patterns can be real without being fundamental or sharply bounded.

Ñāṇananda explains how signs become affectively significant, appropriated and proliferated into a self-involving world.

Together they establish a powerful structural account:

[
\boxed{
\text{nested dynamics}
\rightarrow
\text{stable distinctions}
\rightarrow
\text{actionable objects}
\rightarrow
\text{larger agents}
\rightarrow
\text{personally significant worlds}.
}
]

But they do not establish:

[
\boxed{
\text{why this organization is manifest from a point of view.}
}
]

The surviving primitive is no longer “cognition.”

It is:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{perspectival manifestation}.
}
]

---

# XIV. The new direction

All later Logicvids can begin beyond the discarded question:

> Is cognition a discrete, internally self-validating event?

The live research programme is now:

## Individuation

[
\boxed{
\text{What makes many nested processes one process?}
}
]

## Agency

[
\boxed{
\text{What makes that process pursue goals belonging to the whole?}
}
]

## Perspectivity

[
\boxed{
\text{What makes reality become organized relative to that process?}
}
]

## Manifestation

[
\boxed{
\text{Why does that perspectivally organized world appear?}
}
]

## Śaiva pressure

[
\boxed{
\text{Why is prakāśa intrinsically vimarśa—why does manifestation take the form of active self-articulation at all?}
}
]

This is the point from which the project can begin causing genuine philosophical damage.

Abhinavagupta cannot now be dismissed by explaining perception as prediction, cognition as process, objects as interfaces, or selves as collective biological constructions.

Those explanations can all be absorbed into a more granular account of finite *vimarśa*.

But Abhinavagupta must now answer a harder question than before:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Why should manifestation intrinsically generate nested boundaries, agents, signs, goals and perspectives?}
}
]

And the naturalist must answer the inverse:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{Why should nested boundaries, agents, signs, goals and perspectives ever be manifest?}
}
]

That reciprocal bind is the checkpoint.

The strongest immediate Ñāṇananda branch is: **How does a dynamically useful sign become charged as “for me,” and does the construction of that significance also construct the observer?**

